Cross-File Generic Functions — Sibling Import
==============================================

Per reference §Modules:

> A module may span multiple source files in the same directory.
> Files that declare the same module path share a single
> namespace: declarations in one file are directly visible from
> the others **without an explicit `import` clause**.

This file pins **generic functions** declared in
`generic_helpers.lsysl` and called from here, all without an
`import`. The two files share module path
`sysl.tests.lang_features.generics.siblings`.

Pins the regression fixed at sysl@4f1f81725 ("cross-file
generic-fn body re-analysis"):

  - Sibling-imported generic functions resolve and instantiate at
    explicit type args.
  - Inference works at the call site when the type argument is
    determined by the value argument(s).
  - Two-parameter generic inference works across file boundaries.
  - A generic function returning a generic struct produces an
    instance whose fields are readable at the caller — `linkImported
    GenericStructToTemplate` plumbs the template binding through.
  - An outer generic body calling another sibling-imported generic
    works.
  - A **parameterless** generic-returning function imported from a
    sibling auto-calls on bare reference (was the headline bug: the
    `isParameterless` flag was dropped on the wire, so callers
    treated the symbol as a function pointer and inference broke).

What this file does NOT pin:

- Same-file generic functions (covered by `generic_fn_basic.lsysl`
  in the parent directory).
- Generic type aliases — those live in
  `generic_alias_cross_file.lsysl`.

File-local prefix `Gfsi` (Generic-Fn Sibling-Import).

    module sysl.tests.lang_features.generics.siblings


Explicit type-arg call across the file boundary
-----------------------------------------------

### Generic fn with explicit `[int]` instantiation

    #test
    test_gfsi_explicit_type_arg() -> unit
        val r = gh_id[int](42)
        assert(r == 42, s"gh_id[int](42); got $r")

### Generic fn with explicit `[string]` instantiation

A different concrete instantiation of the same generic — the
analyzer must monomorphize each instance separately and the
cross-file symbol table must serve both at the same call site
context.

    #test
    test_gfsi_explicit_type_arg_string() -> unit
        val r = gh_id[string]("hello")
        assert(r == "hello", s"gh_id[string](\"hello\"); got '$r'")


Type-argument inference across the file boundary
------------------------------------------------

### Inferred type arg — int

The type argument is determined entirely by the value-argument
expression. The sibling-imported `gh_id` must register as
generic in the importing analyzer or this fails with
"'gh_id' is not generic" or a wrong-type error.

    #test
    test_gfsi_inferred_int() -> unit
        val r = gh_id(7)
        assert(r == 7, s"inferred int; got $r")

### Inferred type arg — bool

    #test
    test_gfsi_inferred_bool() -> unit
        val r = gh_id(true)
        assert(r == true, "inferred bool")

### Inferred type arg — string

    #test
    test_gfsi_inferred_string() -> unit
        val r = gh_id("world")
        assert(r == "world", s"inferred string; got '$r'")


Two-parameter generic inference
-------------------------------

### `gh_first[A, B]` inferred from positional args

`gh_first[A, B](a: A, b: B) -> A` — both type variables get bound
from the call's value arguments. The result type is the first
type variable, so the assignment site has a concrete type
flowing back too.

    #test
    test_gfsi_two_param_inferred() -> unit
        val r = gh_first(99, "discarded")
        assert(r == 99, s"first; got $r")

### `gh_first` with explicit type args

    #test
    test_gfsi_two_param_explicit() -> unit
        val r = gh_first[int, string](5, "ignored")
        assert(r == 5, s"explicit; got $r")


Generic function returning a generic struct
-------------------------------------------

`gh_wrap[T](x: T) -> GhBox[T]` exercises the cross-file template
linking. The returned struct's `value` field must be read in this
file via field access (`.value`) — that only works if the
analyzer has linked the imported `GhBox_int` symbol back to its
generic template.

### Read a field of a generic struct returned by a sibling generic

    #test
    test_gfsi_wrap_int_field_read() -> unit
        val b = gh_wrap(11)
        assert(b.value == 11, s"value=${b.value}")

### Round-trip through `gh_wrap` for two different concrete types

The two instantiations live in the importing unit's monomorph
table simultaneously — pin that this works.

    #test
    test_gfsi_wrap_two_instantiations() -> unit
        val bi = gh_wrap(3)
        val bs = gh_wrap("note")
        assert(bi.value == 3, s"int box; got ${bi.value}")
        assert(bs.value == "note", s"string box; got '${bs.value}'")


Generic body calling another sibling-imported generic
-----------------------------------------------------

### `gh_wrap_twice` defined in the helper composes `gh_wrap` and `gh_id`

Both inner calls are themselves sibling-imported generics. The
test only sees the outer; the analyzer has to monomorphize
*through* the imported generic at the inner call sites.

    #test
    test_gfsi_wrap_twice_int() -> unit
        val b = gh_wrap_twice(20)
        assert(b.value == 20, s"value=${b.value}")


Generic with body using arithmetic on the type parameter
--------------------------------------------------------

### `gh_double[int]` returns 2x

`gh_double[T](x: T) -> T = x + x` — body uses `+`. Concrete int
instantiation must compile and run.

    #test
    test_gfsi_double_int() -> unit
        val r = gh_double(21)
        assert(r == 42, s"double; got $r")


Parameterless generic-returning function — bare reference auto-calls
--------------------------------------------------------------------

This is the headline regression of sysl@4f1f81725: a sibling-
imported parameterless function that returns a generic-struct
instance. The bare reference must auto-call and the result must
typecheck as `GhBox[int]`.

### Bare reference reads the box's field

If the parameterless flag is lost on the wire, this either fails
to compile (`gh_zero_box` resolves as `() -> GhBox[int]` and
`.value` is invalid on a function pointer) or compiles but does
not invoke the helper.

    #test
    test_gfsi_parameterless_bare_call() -> unit
        val v = gh_zero_box.value
        assert(v == 0, s"value=${v}")

### Two bare references both auto-call

Pin that the auto-call site is at the use site, not somehow
cached per-symbol — two distinct reads should both produce
fresh `GhBox[int]` instances and both read `.value == 0`.

    #test
    test_gfsi_parameterless_two_reads() -> unit
        val a = gh_zero_box
        val b = gh_zero_box
        assert(a.value == 0, s"a.value=${a.value}")
        assert(b.value == 0, s"b.value=${b.value}")
